callTruthT -- SEQ 669 (Tony's ruling of 2026-11-16, F-160): the truth of a call in `while f();` and in `if x = f();`.
    A.  a loop condition that is a call is tested for truth: `while f();` ends when f hands back a zero count, as on nothing.
    B1. assigning nothing yields nothing: `if x = f();` is false when f hands back nothing.
    A label a rule hands back is true by existing, with or without a value. Not ruled: an empty field is false (B2).

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds the inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this
file's `stop();`, then the row's call written into an if-assign and into a while, on the OLD road (the statement in an
action body, ctGo, defined and called) and on the NEW road (If or WHILe on the statement's text, then testOP). Read on stderr.
    if form      if ctX = CALL; ctR = 1;                                 prints CT if <ctR>       1 = the if read true
    while form   while CALL; { ctN = ctN + 1; if ctN == 3; break; }     prints CT while <ctN>    3 = entered, 0 = not
    CT-H         while CALL; ctN = ctN + 1;   no break: it ends only if the call is read false (the H7 row: it hangs unfixed)

Three facts the rows are built round (measured 2026-11-16, before the cure):
    - an action never hands back nothing: `return null;` and a failed rule returned from an action arrive as a zero count.
      Nothing reaches an assignment only from a rule called directly (CT-7D).
    - on the new road a call needs an argument (INVOKEd wants an expression): `f()` in a STARt text drops the statement in
      silence, so every action here is called with ctR. Not chased.
    - a rule cannot be called from the new road (opCallTest: no method), so the D rows are old road only.

